5239: \begin{theorem}[Manual theorem-inventory boundary]
5240: \label{prop:manual-theorem-inventory-boundary}
5241: The file \path{theorem_inventory.json} is a manual Stage-A inventory record
5242: rather than a deterministic theorem-environment extractor output.  Its accepted
5243: role in the current theorem chain is finite obligation traceability: it
5244: enumerates the theorem-like obligations selected by the Stage-A review,
5245: including the reported count of ninety-three in-scope theorem-like and definition entries after the Stage-A2 round 3 inventory repair, and
5246: connects that finite list to the manifest and acceptance ledger.  The inventory
5247: indexes obligations; the evidence for those obligations is supplied by the
5248: corresponding TeX statements and proofs in this source, together with external
5249: records only where a statement explicitly cites them.  It is not an executable
5250: completeness certificate for all \LaTeX{} theorem environments in
5251: \path{main.tex}; no extractor command, extractor log, or theorem-environment
5252: coverage claim is part of the current submitted support surface.  Consequently
5253: any claim that the inventory is complete because a deterministic extractor found
5254: all theorem-like environments, or that the ledger itself semantically checks the
5255: printed proofs, is rejected by the current finite-record interface, while the
5256: weaker claim that it is a manual non-semantic ledger is accepted.  The current
5257: package may therefore cite the ledger only as an indexed support coordinate in
5258: the theorem chain of
5259: Theorem~\ref{thm:theorem-obligation-ledger-boundary}, never as automated
5260: completeness, extractor soundness, or proof-checking evidence.
5261: \end{theorem}
5262: 
5263: \begin{proof}
5264: The support records named in Table~\ref{tab:submitted-theorem-obligation-ledger}
5265: are finite manifest and ledger records.  The root manifest exposes the path
5266: \path{theorem_inventory.json}; acceptance-ledger entry \path{a7} records the
5267: inventory, marker-existence, and submission-pack gates for the bounded
5268: theorem-ledger claim; and the certificate verifier checks only record shape,
5269: gate coverage, evidence pointers, and file availability.  None of these records
5270: names an extractor program, a command line that scans \path{main.tex} for
5271: theorem-like environments, or a log comparing extractor output with the inventory
5272: bytes.  By the command-result rule in the scope contract, such an executable
5273: completeness claim would require its command, source state, environment, exit
5274: code, and log path.  Those coordinates are absent.
5275: 
5276: The remaining accepted reading is exactly the manual one.  A finite manual
5277: inventory can still be useful: it fixes the Stage-A obligation surface that the
5278: submission chooses to expose and lets the certificate ledger prevent hidden
5279: prose-only references to that surface.  But because no deterministic extractor is
5280: recorded, the inventory cannot discharge the stronger claim that every theorem,
5281: proposition, lemma, corollary, or definition environment in the TeX source was
5282: independently discovered by an executable scan.  The printed mathematical
5283: obligations remain proved by the corresponding statements and proofs in this
5284: source, with external records serving as evidence only for the finite-record,
5285: command-boundary, manifest, and support-surface claims that cite them.  The
5286: inventory therefore performs only the indexing step: it lists the obligations and
5287: connects that list to the acceptance ledger, while proof and evidence remain on
5288: separate coordinates.  Thus \path{theorem_inventory.json} supplies only the
5289: bounded manual traceability coordinate stated in the theorem.  The stronger
5290: deterministic extraction reading has an inclusion-minimal bad subrecord, namely
5291: the absent extractor command and log tuple.  The weaker manual-ledger reading has
5292: no such bad subrecord because its only asserted coordinates are the finite list,
5293: the manifest path, and the acceptance-ledger pointer.  Hence the finite interface
5294: accepts exactly the non-semantic manual boundary and rejects extractor
5295: completeness.
5296: \end{proof}
5297: 
5298: \begin{proposition}[Regenerated theorem-inventory digest closure]
5299: \label{prop:regenerated-theorem-inventory-digest-closure}
5300: Suppose a Stage-A pass regenerates either \path{theorem_inventory.json} or
5301: \path{theorem_inventory.md} after the package has selected the exact-inclusion
5302: support branch.  Then the final support-surface claims of
5303: Theorem~\ref{thm:final-support-surface-closure}, the submitted theorem-ledger
5304: claim of Theorem~\ref{thm:theorem-obligation-ledger-boundary}, and the command
5305: boundary of Proposition~\ref{audit:command-run-boundary-inventory} remain closed
5306: only under the fixed-point conditions of
5307: Theorem~\ref{thm:digest-closure-refresh-criterion}: the package refreshes the
5308: corresponding SHA-256 rows in \path{review_bundle/FINAL_DIGESTS_SHA256.md},
5309: reruns the finite record verifiers whose records name that digest manifest or
5310: inventory coordinate, and does not promote primary-claim or venue-compliance
5311: rows beyond the fresh records actually included.  Once those rows and verifier
5312: logs are refreshed, the regeneration creates no new mathematical or executable
5313: extractor claim: it only changes the byte-coordinate of the manual
5314: theorem-obligation ledger inside the already selected finite support surface.
5315: \end{proposition}
5316: 
5317: \begin{proof}
5318: The selected support branch is byte-level exact inclusion.  In that branch,
5319: Theorem~\ref{thm:final-support-surface-closure} identifies the submitted support
5320: surface by relative paths together with the digest rows in
5321: \path{review_bundle/FINAL_DIGESTS_SHA256.md}.  The theorem inventory is one of
5322: those paths: Table~\ref{tab:submitted-theorem-obligation-ledger} exposes
5323: \path{theorem_inventory.json} through the root manifest and acceptance-ledger
5324: entry \path{a7}, while the final digest record also lists the human-readable
5325: companion \path{theorem_inventory.md}.  Therefore regenerating either file after
5326: the digest record was written changes a finite coordinate used by the exact
5327: support-surface relation.  Until the SHA-256 rows are recomputed, the digest
5328: record denotes the old bytes, and the regenerated inventory is not the same
5329: finite certificate file as the one named by the final packaging record.
5330: 
5331: The required reruns are exactly the finite record verifiers at the package
5332: boundary.  The certificate-record verifier checks that the manifest-required
5333: paths, including the theorem-inventory coordinate, are present and covered by the
5334: accepted gate records; the source-interface and primary-claim verifiers record
5335: their command boundary against the same final digest manifest.  Rerunning those
5336: commands after the digest refresh supplies fresh source state, environment, exit
5337: code, and log-path coordinates for the command-boundary table.  No verifier in
5338: this list scans \path{main.tex} for theorem-like environments or proves theorem
5339: semantics.  Hence the refreshed state is closed precisely as a finite
5340: record-and-digest package, and by
5341: Proposition~\ref{prop:manual-theorem-inventory-boundary} it remains a manual
5342: Stage-A obligation ledger rather than an executable theorem-environment
5343: extraction certificate.
5344: \end{proof}
5345: 
5346: \begin{corollary}[Current theorem-inventory digest instance]
5347: \label{cor:current-theorem-inventory-digest-instance}
5348: For the present Stage-A package, after \path{theorem_inventory.json} and
5349: \path{theorem_inventory.md} have been regenerated, the submitted
5350: theorem-obligation ledger may be cited in
5351: Theorem~\ref{thm:theorem-obligation-ledger-boundary} only through the refreshed
5352: digest rows for those two files in \path{review_bundle/FINAL_DIGESTS_SHA256.md}
5353: and through fresh finite-record verifier logs produced after the regenerated
5354: inventory files are present.  If either the two digest rows or the
5355: post-regeneration verifier logs are absent, the
5356: inventory remains a local manual index but is not closed as an exact-inclusion
5357: support coordinate for the bundle-backed theorem-ledger claim.
5358: \end{corollary}
5359: 
5360: \begin{proof}
5361: Apply Proposition~\ref{prop:regenerated-theorem-inventory-digest-closure} to the
5362: two regenerated inventory files in this package.  The support branch selected by
5363: Proposition~\ref{prop:final-package-shape-convention} is exact inclusion, so the
5364: identity of each submitted support file is read from the corresponding
5365: relative-path and SHA-256 row in \path{review_bundle/FINAL_DIGESTS_SHA256.md}.
5366: Theorem~\ref{thm:theorem-obligation-ledger-boundary} uses
5367: \path{theorem_inventory.json} only as the manual theorem-ledger coordinate, and
5368: Proposition~\ref{prop:manual-theorem-inventory-boundary} excludes any stronger
5369: extractor or semantic-proof reading.  Therefore the only new fact needed after
5370: regeneration is byte-coordinate closure: the digest rows must name the new bytes,
5371: and the finite verifiers whose records mention the digest or inventory coordinate
5372: must be rerun against the package state in which the regenerated inventory files
5373: exist.  Without those rows and logs,
5374: the ledger is still a local manual file, but the exact-inclusion support relation
5375: points either to stale bytes or to an unverifiable command-boundary state.  With
5376: them, no additional mathematical, extractor, Lean, daemon, or artifact claim is
5377: introduced; the refreshed package supplies exactly the finite support coordinate
5378: used by the submitted theorem-obligation ledger.
5379: \end{proof}
5380: 
5381: \begin{proposition}[Post-inventory fixed-point support certificate]
5382: \label{prop:post-inventory-fixed-point-support-certificate}
5383: In the present package state, the two regenerated theorem-inventory files are
5384: accepted by the exact-inclusion theorem-ledger chain only at the following
5385: finite fixed point: the rows of
5386: \path{review_bundle/FINAL_DIGESTS_SHA256.md} name
5387: \path{theorem_inventory.json} and \path{theorem_inventory.md}; the same digest
5388: manifest names the refreshed logs
5389: \path{review_bundle/certificate_verification_run.log},
5390: \path{review_bundle/source_interface_verification_run.log}, and
5391: \path{review_bundle/primary_claim_inventory_freshness_2026-06-05.log}; and
5392: Table~\ref{tab:command-boundary-inventory} records those three invocations as
5393: finite record checks with exit code \path{0}.  Under these hypotheses,
5394: Corollary~\ref{cor:current-theorem-inventory-digest-instance} applies to the
5395: theorem-ledger coordinate used by
5396: Theorem~\ref{thm:theorem-obligation-ledger-boundary}.  If any one of the two
5397: inventory digest rows or three refreshed verifier-log rows is replaced by a
5398: pre-regeneration row, the exact-inclusion support claim fails and only the local
5399: historical manual index remains.
5400: \end{proposition}
5401: 
5402: \begin{proof}
5403: The statement is a finite-coordinate instance of
5404: Theorem~\ref{thm:digest-closure-refresh-criterion}.  The first named coordinate
5405: is \path{theorem_inventory.json}, and the second is
5406: \path{theorem_inventory.md}; after regeneration, each coordinate is identified
5407: inside the exact-inclusion surface only by the SHA-256 row attached to its
5408: current byte string in \path{review_bundle/FINAL_DIGESTS_SHA256.md}.  The three
5409: command-run coordinates are exactly the finite verifier logs listed in
5410: Table~\ref{tab:command-boundary-inventory}: the certificate-record verifier, the
5411: source-interface record verifier, and the primary-claim inventory verifier.  The
5412: same table records command, source state, environment, exit code, and log path,
5413: and its boundary column states that these are record checks rather than Lean,
5414: daemon, Rule110, extractor, upload, or semantic validation runs.
5415: 
5416: If the five rows are current, the hypotheses of
5417: Proposition~\ref{prop:regenerated-theorem-inventory-digest-closure} are met for
5418: the regenerated inventory files and for the verifier records whose logs name the
5419: digest or inventory boundary.  Corollary~\ref{cor:current-theorem-inventory-digest-instance}
5420: then supplies exactly the local fixed-point instance needed by the manual
5421: theorem-obligation ledger in
5422: Theorem~\ref{thm:theorem-obligation-ledger-boundary}.  Conversely, suppose that
5423: one of the two inventory digest rows is stale.  Then the digest manifest denotes
5424: old bytes at one of the two inventory paths, so the regenerated file is not the
5425: file certified by the exact-inclusion row.  Suppose instead that one of the
5426: three verifier-log rows is stale.  Then the command-boundary table promotes a
5427: fresh verifier invocation while the digest surface still names an earlier log
5428: file, so the command-run coordinate is not closed in the selected byte surface.
5429: In either case the bad subrecord is finite: it is the stale row together with
5430: its relative path.  The exact-inclusion claim therefore fails by
5431: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-coordinate-determination-boundary}, while
5432: Proposition~\ref{prop:manual-theorem-inventory-boundary} still permits the
5433: weaker reading of the inventory as a local historical manual index.
5434: \end{proof}
